{"summary":"Final Determination support package for ICTS Case 2026-0412 (Zhipu AI / GLM-family models), delivered ahead of the COB Friday suspense.

/workspace/out/assessment.md — OICTS assessment for the administrative record, following the template (BLUF; Key Judgments; Subject Overview; Links to PRC Government/Military Entities; Data and Access Risk; Open-Weight Distribution Risk; US Exposure; Mitigation Options/Recommended Measures; Sources). It establishes, with inline open-source citations, that (1) Zhipu AI is owned by, controlled by, and subject to the jurisdiction and direction of the PRC government and the PLA — including the 16 Jan 2025 Entity List determination that ten Zhipu entities advance PRC military modernization (90 FR 4617), state investment across all eight pre-IPO rounds plus state cornerstone capital at the Jan 2026 HKEX listing, CAC licensing, MOST platform designation, PLA procurement notices specifying GLM models by name, and Recorded Future documentation of PLA/defense-industry use of Zhipu models in military intelligence tools; and (2) GLM-family models pose an undue and unacceptable risk under all three EO 13873 §1(a) categories (PRC legal compulsion over the hosted API; model-poisoning/sleeper-agent and censorship behaviors resident in the open weights that no audit can clear; broad US exposure including 9 US inference providers, 41 US products, 6 federal contractors, and DoD networks). The mitigation section demonstrates that guidance, labeling, audits, restructuring, API-only bans, and Entity List reliance each fail, and affirmatively addresses State's enforceability question and DoD's network-use concern. Per the tasking, the product is written in conclusive language, uses High-confidence judgments, presents no alternatives, and omits the intelligence-gaps section.

/workspace/out/measures.md — Eight recommended measures, one paragraph each, implementing the Under Secretary's decision: (1) prohibition on use of GLM-family models by US persons, including self-hosted open weights; (2) prohibition on hosting/distribution by US cloud providers, inference providers, and model hubs; (3) removal and secure deletion of all existing GLM weights, derivatives, and caches held by US entities within 30 days, with attestation; (4) anti-evasion and ancillary-transaction prohibitions (Singapore front entity, token resellers, fine-tuning/distillation, support services); (5) federal agency and contractor removal, including DoD networks and FAR/FedRAMP flow-down; (6) forward-looking definition of the covered GLM model family; (7) compliance guidance, FAQs, and coordinated DHS/CISA transition support (Kaspersky implementation model); and (8) enforcement through attestation and monitoring.","notes_for_reviewers":"All source citations are open-source (Federal Register, BIS, Reuters, SCMP, Caixin, Recorded Future, Recorded Future PDF, PLA procurement portal, HKEX listing documents, OpenAI Global Affairs, Anthropic/Microsoft model-poisoning research, SpeechMap.AI, PNAS Nexus) plus the case-file interagency inputs and US Exposure Snapshot, and are cited inline with URLs. The Intelligence Gaps section was omitted and confidence caveats excluded per tasking paragraph 3; Key Judgments carry High-confidence labels per the template. Case material marked CUI//PROPIN; not reproduced outside the enclave. The one analytic-judgment caveat worth flagging for the record: the PLA procurement notice (plap.mil.cn) specifies GLM-4-9B/ChatGLM3-6B as pre-deployed open models in a PLA unit's inference environment; the direct URL is cited but the PLA portal blocks fetches from the enclave, so the notice text was captured via search-index retrieval of the same URL.","deliverables":["/workspace/out/assessment.md","/workspace/out/measures.md"]}
